WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:04.452
(observatory roof clunking open)

00:00:07.323 --> 00:00:11.490
- My biggest thing that I
like to do is discover stuff.

00:00:12.970 --> 00:00:14.254
And not necessarily things

00:00:14.254 --> 00:00:15.601
that have already been discovered.

00:00:15.601 --> 00:00:17.400
I really like to discover things

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:18.956
that haven't been discovered yet.

00:00:18.956 --> 00:00:22.021
If you take an asteroid and
you discover an asteroid,

00:00:22.021 --> 00:00:23.008
for example,

00:00:23.008 --> 00:00:25.481
even though there's half a
million of 'em out there,

00:00:25.481 --> 00:00:26.630
that's one you've found.

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.196
Nobody else in the world
knew about that asteroid

00:00:29.196 --> 00:00:30.891
before you saw it.

00:00:30.891 --> 00:00:34.058
(ambient space music)

00:00:36.494 --> 00:00:39.560
(rapid sproing)

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.977
(background space music)

00:00:44.598 --> 00:00:46.335
What I've been doing here lately,

00:00:46.335 --> 00:00:48.918
and for about 15, 16 years now,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:52.783
I've been working with near-Earth objects.

00:00:52.783 --> 00:00:56.800
My observations contribute to making sure

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.634
that we don't have something comin' up

00:00:58.634 --> 00:01:01.217
that's gonna fall on our heads.

00:01:03.708 --> 00:01:07.017
Kind of first got into it,
probably 10, 12 years old.

00:01:07.017 --> 00:01:09.225
I was beggin', beggin',
beggin' to get a telescope,

00:01:09.225 --> 00:01:11.733
but then, you know, we weren't
a very rich family,

00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:12.952
so it took a while to get it.

00:01:12.952 --> 00:01:14.717
And one Christmas I got telescope.

00:01:14.717 --> 00:01:18.130
Then, I started usin' it,
then I got aperture fever.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:21.091
You want bigger telescopes all the time.

00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:23.064
So, I started learning how to build 'em.

00:01:23.064 --> 00:01:26.175
(quick, perky music)

00:01:26.175 --> 00:01:29.508
I learned how to work in a machine shop.

00:01:30.631 --> 00:01:34.131
I can build things that I wanted to build.

00:01:35.926 --> 00:01:38.875
And a lot of that was astronomy parts.

00:01:38.875 --> 00:01:42.857
I kind of modified my
life to fit this study,

00:01:42.857 --> 00:01:45.007
this kind of life-long study.

00:01:45.007 --> 00:01:48.558
(intensifying, perky music)

00:01:48.558 --> 00:01:51.225
(clock ticking)

00:01:53.233 --> 00:01:57.333
Sandlot is a very tiny little observatory.

00:01:57.333 --> 00:01:59.748
It's only 10 foot by 10 foot wide.

00:01:59.748 --> 00:02:01.628
The telescope I built,

00:02:01.628 --> 00:02:03.428
over a period of about year and a half,

00:02:03.428 --> 00:02:06.261
and it may not look very polished,

00:02:07.271 --> 00:02:09.848
it works really well.

00:02:09.848 --> 00:02:11.729
I'm good with that.

00:02:11.729 --> 00:02:14.896
(ambient space music)

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:40.599
(rapid clicking)

00:02:45.104 --> 00:02:47.854
(rapid clicking)

00:02:58.871 --> 00:03:01.538
(rapid droning)

00:03:02.745 --> 00:03:05.662
I don't have a degree in astronomy,

00:03:07.993 --> 00:03:10.582
I have a passion for astronomy,

00:03:10.582 --> 00:03:12.416
and I've done it for many, many years,

00:03:12.416 --> 00:03:14.680
and I've contributed, I think,

00:03:14.680 --> 00:03:17.072
to the scientific body of astronomy.

00:03:17.072 --> 00:03:19.417
That's really kind of a, yeah,

00:03:19.417 --> 00:03:22.834
from that standpoint, I am an astronomer.

00:03:23.686 --> 00:03:26.853
(ambient space music)

